<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE IV: </span>Zero-shot grounded VideoQA on NExT-GQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>. Here, Acc@IoP=0.5 is also termed Acc@GQA. <sup id="S4.T4.10" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.T4.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">†</span></sup> indicates our evaluation using the official checkpoint. Note that VideoMind’s training data includes the NExT-GQA training set, so its performance is not strictly zero-shot.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Comparison with State-of-the-Art</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ IV-B Implementation Details ‣ IV Experiments ‣ VideoTG-R1: Boosting Video Temporal Grounding via Curriculum Reinforcement Learning on Reflected Boundary Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>, <a href="#S4.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ IV-B Implementation Details ‣ IV Experiments ‣ VideoTG-R1: Boosting Video Temporal Grounding via Curriculum Reinforcement Learning on Reflected Boundary Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, <a href="#S4.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ IV-B Implementation Details ‣ IV Experiments ‣ VideoTG-R1: Boosting Video Temporal Grounding via Curriculum Reinforcement Learning on Reflected Boundary Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, and <a href="#S4.T4" title="TABLE IV ‣ IV-B Implementation Details ‣ IV Experiments ‣ VideoTG-R1: Boosting Video Temporal Grounding via Curriculum Reinforcement Learning on Reflected Boundary Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>, VideoTG-R1 achieves state-of-the-art performance across these four benchmarks. Specifically, on Charades-STA and ANet, VideoTG-R1 outperforms previous SoTA by margins of 5.7% and 4.0% on mIoU, respectively.
For the grounded VideoQA task, VideoTG-R1 outperforms VideoMind <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> on RexTime by 4.63% in mIoU and 5.66% in Acc@IoU@0.5, and VideoTG-R1 outperforms VideoChat-TPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> on NExT-GQA by 6.5% in mIoU and 1.0% in Acc@IoP@0.5.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, these results highlight that the grounding timestamps generated by our VideoTG-R1 are of higher quality.</p>
</div>
